# apps/api — HTTP and worker modes. Copy to .env for a host run; the Compose stack supplies
# these as service environment (infra/compose).

# Which entrypoint main.ts dispatches to. 'worker' consumes the queue and serves no HTTP.
MODE=http
PORT=3000

# DR-1 / NFR-1: with this false, UC-17…48 must still pass end to end.
BILLING_ENABLED=true

# NOTE — the one setting that catches everyone. config/configuration.ts defaults DB_HOST to
# 'postgres' and REDIS_HOST to 'redis', which are the Compose SERVICE NAMES. Running apps/api
# on the host means overriding both to localhost, as below; running it inside Compose means
# leaving them as the service names. Backwards produces ENOTFOUND postgres, which reads as a
# broken stack rather than a wrong .env.
DB_HOST=localhost
DB_PORT=5432
DB_NAME=esg

# Non-superuser, non-owner, RLS NOT bypassable (AD-2). Must match ESG_APP_PASSWORD in
# infra/compose/.env.
DB_USER=esg_app
DB_PASSWORD=devonly-app

# The worker's role (§7.6). Read only when MODE=worker, so one local .env serves both entrypoints
# without editing between runs. In production the worker container simply sets DB_USER=esg_worker
# and these are unset, which keeps the worker's credential out of the api's environment.
#
# It is not interchangeable with esg_app: the dispatcher must SELECT and UPDATE audit.outbox_event,
# which esg_app may only INSERT into. Running the worker as esg_app fails on the first poll.
DB_WORKER_USER=esg_worker
DB_WORKER_PASSWORD=devonly-worker

# The migration owner (§7.6), used by `pnpm db:migrate` / `db:revert` and by nothing else.
# Read straight from the environment by infrastructure/persistence/migration.data-source.ts,
# which is the one place in this app permitted to bypass ConfigService — it is loaded by the
# TypeORM CLI, outside Nest, where no ConfigService exists.
#
# THIS IS LOCAL-ONLY AND MUST STAY THAT WAY. §7.7 makes the append-only guarantee depend on
# these credentials never being available to a runtime process: an owner can ALTER TABLE ...
# DISABLE TRIGGER or drop it outright. In staging and production the migration job carries
# them and the api/worker containers do not — never add them to a runtime service's
# environment to "keep the files consistent".
DB_MIGRATOR_USER=esg_migrator
DB_MIGRATOR_PASSWORD=devonly-migrator

REDIS_HOST=localhost
REDIS_PORT=6379

# ── Identity (task 19) ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

# architecture.md §9.1: "Argon2id, per-user salt, pepper from secret manager". Deliberately NOT
# defaulted in code — a hash computed without it is not the hash §9.1 specifies, and a default
# would make that indistinguishable from a correct one. The HTTP tier throws at boot without it.
#
# Changing it makes every existing password unverifiable, so it is per-environment and permanent.
# This synthetic value is for the local stack; production takes it from the secret manager (NFR-69).
AUTH_PASSWORD_PEPPER=devonly-pepper

# AD-12's access-token signing secret (task 21). HTTP tier only, like the pepper, and likewise
# never defaulted in code — the tier throws at boot without it. Unlike the pepper it CAN be
# rotated: every outstanding access token dies within its 15-minute lifetime and refresh tokens
# are database rows, so rotation costs one forced refresh, not any data.
AUTH_JWT_SECRET=devonly-jwt-secret

# Which EmailPort adapter to register (architecture.md §8.1, §12.5.2). No default, on purpose:
# `log` writes the recipient and the verification link into the application log, which NFR-30
# forbids of a production pipeline — so a deployment that has not chosen a provider fails at boot
# rather than logging personal data for months. Mailjet (OQ-12) is registered by task 51.
#
# Read by the WORKER only: mail is sent from the outbox, never from the request tier (AD-6, AD-10).
EMAIL_PROVIDER=log

# Origin of apps/web, used to build the verification link the email carries. Never derived from a
# request Host header — the worker composing the mail has no request, and a link built from Host is
# a redirect-poisoning path. 3100 is `next dev --port 3100`.
PUBLIC_WEB_URL=http://localhost:3100

# The admin realm's one secret (task 23, §12.5.6): the HS256 signing key and the session
# cookie's AES-256-GCM sealing key are both HKDF-derived from it under distinct labels, so one
# rotation retires both. Deliberately separate from AUTH_JWT_SECRET — NFR-65's "no shared
# credential" includes the signing key, and disjoint secrets are what make a tenant access token
# structurally unable to pass for an admin one. HTTP tier only; the worker holds none of these.
AUTH_ADMIN_SECRET=devonly-admin-secret

# The console's exact origin: what CORS allows with credentials, and what the Origin proof on
# state-changing admin-realm requests compares against (§12.5.6, task 23). 3200 is apps/admin's
# dev port.
ADMIN_ORIGIN=http://localhost:3200

# Social sign-in's environment half (task 24, §12.5.6's task-24 configuration row): per-provider
# client secrets. Everything else about a provider — enabled state, client id, issuer, scopes,
# redirect allowlist — is configuration-store data (config/seed/identity-provider.*.json), edited
# without a redeploy. A missing secret makes that ONE provider unavailable (logged at error); it
# does not stop the tier. Rotation is an environment change until OpenBao exists — the recorded
# FR-82 deferral. These synthetic values serve the e2e suite's stub provider; real deployments
# hold real secrets, in the secret manager once it exists (NFR-69).
AUTH_SOCIAL_GOOGLE_CLIENT_SECRET=devonly-google-secret
AUTH_SOCIAL_MICROSOFT_CLIENT_SECRET=devonly-microsoft-secret

# Permits http:// OIDC issuers — the e2e suite's stub provider listens on localhost over http.
# Anything but the exact string 'true' leaves TLS required. NEVER set in production; an https
# issuer is unaffected either way, so the flag cannot weaken a real provider.
AUTH_SOCIAL_ALLOW_INSECURE=true
